<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Principate</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Principate is the term for the first period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, from Augustus in 27 BCE until the late third century CE. Emperors ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a>, maintaining the outward forms of republican institutions. It gave way to a more openly autocratic system under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominate">Dominate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term comes from the position of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a> senatus, the most influential member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> in the Republic, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> abolished and Augustus revived in 28 BCE. Princeps, meaning leading or first citizen, had applied to nobles who distinguished themselves in service to the Republic. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Battle of Actium</a> and the defeat of Antony and Cleopatra, Octavian gained power step by step while courting the Senate and the people and presenting himself as a restorer of the Republic.</p><p>On 13 January 27 BCE he made a show of returning power to the Senate. On 16 January the Senate gave him the title augustus and asked him to take command of the provinces again, which he accepted for ten years, and with them control of most of the legions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Historians Werner Eck and Sarolta Takács trace the authority Augustus created to three sources: powers of office delegated by the Senate and people, his immense private fortune, and the patron-client relationships he built across the empire. Together these formed his auctoritas. After a second settlement in 23 BCE, when he gave up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, he held a proconsular imperium greater than that of all other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proconsul">proconsuls</a> throughout the empire and the power of a tribune for life, which let him convene the Senate and people and veto their actions. In 12 BCE he became pontifex maximus, a title that emperors kept until 476 CE.</p><p>The forms of oligarchic self-rule inherited from the Republic, under the motto of the Senate and People of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, masked the reality of autocratic rule. After the fall of the Julio-Claudian dynasty in 68 CE, Vespasian gave the position a legal basis in the lex quae dicitur de imperio Vespasiani, and the military role of the imperator gradually overshadowed the civilian ideal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The nineteenth-century German scholar Theodor Mommsen proposed the periodisation of Principate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominate">Dominate</a>. He saw the Principate as a constitutional system, a diarchy in which power was shared between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a> and the Senate, that broke down in the third century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>. Since the early twentieth century scholars have increasingly rejected this view.</p><p>Historians following Ronald Syme regard the Principate as a variant of monarchy, and they no longer find diarchy a useful concept. Jochen Bleicken considers the scheme unsuitable because its legal basis is flawed and it suggests that the earlier imperial period was freer. The term is nonetheless still used for the first phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, from Augustus to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crisis-of-the-third-century">Crisis of the Third Century</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Principate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Principate</b>'s constitutional fiction gave way under Diocletian (r. 284–305 CE) to an openly autocratic model, the Dominate, in which the emperor was addressed as dominus (lord) and surrounded by elaborate court ceremonial.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 27 BCE he formally restored the Republic to the Senate and people and received the name Augustus, keeping real power and founding the <b>Principate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
